From 327fa6d2fc5c54a370c781a844ec9d03146a69e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carson Daniel <wcarsond@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 00:42:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] vulkan : two-device all-reduce without a CPU stop
 (relay thread)

The one-stop all-reduce still drained both devices once per exchange. Now each
device signals a timeline semaphore of its own when its slot is written and
when it has read the other's slot, and the other device waits in-queue on a
mirror semaphore; a relay thread polls the source counters and host-signals
the mirrors. The main thread never waits and neither device drains. Host slots
are a ring sized from a 64 MB per-device budget (2..256 slots); the "read"
signals guard reuse, so any depth >= 2 is correct. A failed reservation is
remembered rather than retried every exchange.

Vulkan's external semaphores would do this without the thread, and the Intel
Windows driver reports them exportable and importable for timeline semaphores,
but an import between the two physical devices succeeds and shares nothing:
host and queue signals on one device are never seen on the other (probe in
llama.cpp-b60/vk-semaphore-probe). GGML_VK_COMM_SYNC=1 keeps the one-stop
path for A/B.

Measured on two Arc Pro B60s, Windows 11, vs one card (tg128 / pp512):
  granite 30B dense     one-stop 1.11x / 1.47x -> no-stop 1.24x / 1.49x
  qwen2.5-coder 14B     one-stop 0.92x / 1.25x -> no-stop 1.03x / 1.26x
  qwen3 30B-A3B MoE     one-stop 0.49x / 1.20x -> no-stop 0.63x / 1.23x
Output is byte-identical to the generic path on a 500-token greedy completion.
